'''''Cooperativa Unida v. Nurra Provincial Government''''' (1984–1985) was a {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which Cooperativa Unida, an agricultural {{w|cooperative}} of Black Balboan farmers in [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], challenged the provincial government's allocation of agricultural subsidies. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in favour of the cooperative, finding that Nurra Province's subsidy allocation formula systematically disadvantaged Black Balboan-majority cooperatives. The ruling was significant for extending the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' anti-discrimination provisions to provincial economic policy and for establishing that subsidy allocation formulas could constitute indirect {{w|racial discrimination}}.

== Background ==

[[Nurra Province|Nurra]] was one of Balboa's most productive agricultural provinces, with a cooperative farming sector that included both Black Balboan-majority and white/Creole-majority cooperatives. The provincial government administered an agricultural subsidy programme funded through the national budget, providing grants for equipment purchases, irrigation infrastructure, and crop development.

The subsidy allocation formula used by the Nurra provincial government weighted applications based on "land tenure security," a criterion that measured the length and formality of the applicant's land tenure. In practice, Black Balboan farmers, who had acquired land through the post-reunification land redistribution programme, had shorter tenure histories and less formal title documentation than white and Creole farmers, whose land holdings predated reunification. The "land tenure security" criterion therefore systematically disadvantaged Black Balboan-majority cooperatives, which received lower subsidy allocations per hectare than white-majority cooperatives.

Cooperativa Unida was a cooperative of 120 Black Balboan farmers in central Nurra. In 1983, the cooperative applied for a subsidy to purchase irrigation equipment and was awarded 40 per cent of the amount requested. A white-majority cooperative of similar size in the same district, which had applied for a comparable subsidy, was awarded 85 per cent of the amount requested.

== Case ==

Cooperativa Unida brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 1984, arguing that the "land tenure security" criterion was a proxy for racial {{w|discrimination}}. The cooperative's legal team presented a statistical analysis of the province's subsidy allocations over the preceding five years, demonstrating that Black Balboan-majority cooperatives received on average 45 per cent less in subsidy per hectare than white-majority cooperatives, and that the disparity correlated almost exactly with the "land tenure security" weighting.

The Nurra provincial government defended the criterion on the grounds that land tenure security was a legitimate measure of the applicant's ability to maintain long-term agricultural investment and that the criterion was applied uniformly regardless of the applicant's race. The government argued that the disparity reflected historical differences in land acquisition, not discrimination.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled in Cooperativa Unida's favour in 1985, finding that the "land tenure security" criterion constituted indirect {{w|racial discrimination}} under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The Tribunal held that the criterion, while facially neutral, produced a racially disparate outcome that was a direct consequence of the historical discrimination that had prevented Black Balboans from owning land prior to reunification. The Tribunal rejected the government's argument that historical differences justified the disparity, holding that the Guarantees' purpose was to remedy the effects of historical discrimination, not to perpetuate them.

The Tribunal ordered Nurra Province to revise its subsidy allocation formula to eliminate the "land tenure security" criterion and to allocate subsidies based on objective criteria including crop yield, farm size, and demonstrated need. The Tribunal also ordered retroactive adjustments for Black Balboan-majority cooperatives that had been disadvantaged by the formula during the preceding five years.

== Significance ==

The Cooperativa Unida ruling was significant for establishing that the [[Twelve Guarantees]]' anti-discrimination provisions extended to provincial economic policy and that subsidy allocation formulas could constitute indirect discrimination. The ruling built on the disparate impact precedents established in ''[[Vargas v. Sant Cristòfor Port Authority]]'' (1963) and ''[[Desramé v. Estret Provincial Housing Board]]'' (1972) and extended the principle to a new domain: the allocation of public funds.

The ruling was also significant for its rejection of the "historical differences" defence. The Tribunal's holding that the Guarantees' purpose was to remedy the effects of historical discrimination, not to perpetuate them, established an important principle that was cited in subsequent cases involving the legacy of pre-reunification racial inequality.

